    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI consolidated all owner duplicate detection into a single derived `identityKey` and made it the sole basis for the 409 rejection.\n\n**The key (`Owner.getIdentityKey()`)** \u2014 a transient getter on the domain model:\n```\nnormalizedTelephone + \"|\" + (email or \"\") + \"|\" + (householdId or \"\")\n```\nPlaced on `Owner` alongside the other derived getters (`getLocality`, `getDisplayName`, \u2026) so MapStruct maps it onto the DTO automatically, and so both the response and the uniqueness check use one definition \u2014 no duplication.\n\n**Single check** \u2014 new `OwnerIdentityUniquenessValidator` rejects a create only when the new owner's *whole* `identityKey` equals an existing owner's, throwing `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException` (mapped to 409 by a new handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`).\n\n**Removed** the three superseded pieces and their handlers/imports: `OwnerTelephoneUniquenessValidator`, `OwnerEmailUniquenessValidator`, `OwnerHouseholdUniquenessValidator`, and the `DuplicateOwner{Telephone,Email,Household}Exception` classes.\n\n**Controller flow** (`addOwner`) now assigns the household id (when `sharesHousehold` is set) *before* computing/checking the identity key, so household members with different telephones get distinct keys and are both allowed \u2014 while an exact full-key match is still rejected. `OwnerHouseholdRegistrar` (and the `HouseholdKey` it uses) is retained since it's what actually assigns `householdId`.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 added the read-only `identityKey` property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` (so it's returned in responses) and updated the `sharesHousehold` documentation to reflect the new semantics.\n\nBehaviour change, as specified: two owners with the same last name + address but different telephones (and not opted into a shared household) are now both allowed, since their identity keys differ \u2014 only an exact whole-key collision is a duplicate.",
